  "quadrant_html": "<p><b>Reading \u03b1 and \u03b2 together.</b> The green quadrant (\u03b2 &lt; 1, \u03b1 &gt; 0) is the regime you want \u2014 de-risked and still outperforming a \u03b2-scaled passive; the red quadrant (\u03b2 &gt; 1, \u03b1 &lt; 0) is the one to fix \u2014 amplified market exposure that also underperforms its own benchmark.</p><table class='quadrant'><thead><tr><th></th><th>\u03b1 &gt; 0</th><th>\u03b1 &lt; 0</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>\u03b2 &gt; 1</th><td class='q-neut'>Leveraged market exposure <em>and</em> outperforming what that leverage would predict. Acceptable if you wanted the risk.</td><td class='q-bad'><strong>Worst case.</strong> Amplified market exposure that <em>also</em> underperforms its own benchmark \u2014 two drags stacking.</td></tr><tr><th>\u03b2 &lt; 1</th><td class='q-good'><strong>Best case.</strong> De-risked <em>and</em> still outperforming a \u03b2-scaled passive. Best risk-adjusted quadrant.</td><td class='q-neut'>De-risked but lagging even the lower benchmark \u2014 a small drag (\u2248100-150 bps/yr) is often just cash + fees; larger gaps are real underperformance.</td></tr></tbody></table>"
}
